    {
      "id": "hihomo-full",
      "file": "hihomo-full.m4a",
      "text": "This paper asks a simple question: can we make a sample much larger without changing its shape? Expansion microscopy helps us see small structures by physically expanding a sample. However, a large expansion factor alone does not tell us whether the expansion is uniform. The researchers developed a method called high-fold homogeneous expansion microscopy. They reported about eight- to nine-fold expansion in a single round and tested structural preservation in centrioles and nuclear pore complexes. The method also supports labeling after expansion. The main point is to check both how much a sample expands and how well its structure is preserved.",
      "voice": "Ava (Enhanced)",
      "language": "en-US",
      "rate": 175,
      "seconds": 40.833288,
